courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completion of the course, students should have achieved the following outcomes:
(1) understand the fundamentals of prototype and product design;
(2) know how to create models of mechanical components using CAD softwares;
(3) demonstrate the capability of using appropriate manufacturing techniques;
(4) employ engineering and scientific principles to execute a design from concept to finished product;
(5) work as a team to complete a design project.

Upon completion of the course, students should have achieved the following outcomes:
(1) deepen their understanding of topics related to mechanical and/or automation engineering, which may include mechanics, design, materials, fluid mechanics, thermodynamics, control, mechatronics and robotics;
(2) develop practical hands-on skills related to the topics of mechanical and automation engineering;
(3) using computer-aided tools to study and analyze mechanical systems;
(4) demonstrate effective engineering communication skills, including mathematical, verbal and visual means.

Upon completion of the course, students should have achieved the following outcomes:
(1) identify state-of-the-art topics in robotics;
(2) generate new ideas for robot research and development;
(3) carry out project-based learning and research in robotics;
(4) understand impacts of robotics technology.

Upon completion of the course, students should have achieved the following outcomes:
(1) apply fundamental concepts learned in this course in heat transfer and fluid mechanics analysis;
(2) understand energy transport phenomena in micro and nanostructured materials;
(3) use the principles of thermochemistry, fluid dynamics, and chemical kinetics to understand the basic energy conversion process;
(4) understand and apply the basic principles of computational fluid dynamics in analyzing fluid flow problems.

This course contributes to the following EEEN / MAEG Programme Learning Outcomes:
(1) E1: Acquired basic knowledge related to energy and environmental engineering, including physics, mathematics, thermodynamics, energy technologies, power systems, environmental economics, building control and assessment, air pollution, etc. (K,S);
(2) E4: Acquired the ability to function as a member of an interdisciplinary team (S,V);
(3) E5: Acquired the ability to identify, formulate and solve real-life engineering problems and tradeoffs related to energy system and optimization, environmental monitoring and control, and green building technologies (K,S);
(4) E7: Developed proficiency in technical communication (both oral and written) (S);
(5) E10: Acquired the ability of independent and lifelong learning (V);
(6) E11: Acquired the ability to use the techniques, skills and tools to practice in the energy and environmental engineering discipline and be aware of entrepreneur opportunities (K,S);
(7) E12: Acquired the ability to use the computer/IT tools relevant to the discipline along with an understanding of their processes and limitations (K,S,V);
(8) M1: An ability to apply knowledge of engineering sciences and mathematics to the discipline of mechanical and automation engineering;
(9) M4: An ability to function on teams;
(10) M5: An ability to identify, formulate, and solve engineering problems;
(11) M7: An ability to communicate effectively;
(12) M10: An ability to recognize the need for and to engage in life-long learning;
(13) M11: An ability to use the techniques, skills and tools to practice in the mechanical and automation engineering discipline;
(14) M12: An ability to use the computer/IT tools relevant to the discipline along with an understanding of their processes and limitations.

K = Knowledge outcomes; S = Skills outcomes; V = Values and attitude outcomes.

Upon completion of the course, students should have achieved the following outcomes:

1. To appreciate the development of the railway as one of the essential modes of transportation in the global setting;
2. To understand the modern railway transportation technologies and engineering considerations;
3. To appreciate train and railway systems design and technologies, and their impact to the environment;
4. To understand the railway development planning process and considerations; and
5. To understand railway operation and maintenance, safety concerns and risk management.
